        "completedSourceTexts": [
          {
            "textId": "completed-block:paper-gaokao-2022-zhejiang-jun:grammar-fill:1",
            "paragraphId": "block:paper-gaokao-2022-zhejiang-jun:grammar-fill:1",
            "text": "To understand a painting, we’re taught to look for color, composition, and light. But how can a painting be appreciated by someone who’s blind? Through touch, the one thing gallery signs tell you not to do. John Olson, a former photographer and his team turn paintings into fully textured 3D models."
          },
          {
            "textId": "completed-block:paper-gaokao-2022-zhejiang-jun:grammar-fill:2",
            "paragraphId": "block:paper-gaokao-2022-zhejiang-jun:grammar-fill:2",
            "text": "The tactile(可触知的)paintings work as a way to show art to the blind because we don’t see with just our eyes: We see with our brains. Research in the field of neuroplasticity-the brain’s adaptability-shows that the visual cortex(大脑皮层)is made active by touch. Blind people recognize shapes with their existing senses, in a way similar to that of sighted people, says Ella Striem-Amit, a Harvard scientist."
          },
          {
            "textId": "completed-block:paper-gaokao-2022-zhejiang-jun:grammar-fill:3",
            "paragraphId": "block:paper-gaokao-2022-zhejiang-jun:grammar-fill:3",
            "text": "Luc Gandarias, who’s now thirteen, went blind suddenly at age seven. When he felt a 3D version of Leonardo da Vinci’s “Mona Lisa” he noticed her smile right away. \"I can actually feel what you see when you look at it,” he said."
          },
          {
            "textId": "completed-block:paper-gaokao-2022-zhejiang-jun:grammar-fill:4",
            "paragraphId": "block:paper-gaokao-2022-zhejiang-jun:grammar-fill:4",
            "text": "For Luc, this means independence. \"The feeling of being able to see it and to form my opinion is like breaking down another wall as a blind person.”"
          }
        ],
